pt\nprivate checkCircular(cell: string, deps: Set<string>): void {\n  // Check if there's a path from any dep back to cell\n  // by following existing dependency edges\n  const visited = new Set<string>();\n  \n  function dfs(current: string): boolean {\n    if (current === cell) return true; // Found a path back to cell\n    if (visited.has(current)) return false;\n    visited.add(current);\n    \n    // Follow dependency edges from current\n    // current depends on...?\n    // Actually, I need to follow edges in the direction: current → (what current depends on)\n    // Wait no, I need to go from dep downward to see if we reach cell\n    \n    // Hmm, let me reconsider.\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOK actually I'm overcomplicating this. Let me define:\n\ndependency graph tracks: `dependencies[X]` = set of cells that X depends on.\n\nEdge direction: X → Y means X depends on Y.\n\nCurrently: B1 → A1 (B1 depends on A1)\nProposed: A1 → B1 (A1 depends on B1)\n\nCycle check: after adding A1 → B1, does the graph have a cycle?\n- Do a DFS from A1. Can we reach A1 again?\n  - From A1, we go to B1 (A1's dependency)\n  - From B1, we go to A1 (B1's dependency)\n  - We reached A1! Cycle detected.\n\nSo the algorithm is:\n1. Temporarily add the new dependencies\n2. Do a DFS from `cell`, following dependency edges\n3. If we encounter `cell` again, there's a cycle\n4. Remove temporary edges\n\nOr more efficiently, check before adding:\n- Starting from each `dep` in dependencies, follow dependency edges\n- If we reach `cell`, adding would create a cycle\n\nWait, that's BFS/DFS from `dep` following the direction `dep → (dependencies of dep) → ...`. If we reach `cell`, then `dep → ... → cell` exists in the current graph. Adding `cell → dep` would complete the cycle.\n\nSo:\n```\nfunction wouldCreateCycle(cell, deps):\n  for each dep in deps:\n    // BFS/DFS from dep following dependency edges\n    if reachable(dep, cell): return true\n  return false\n```\n\nWhere reachable(start, target) does a