c/index.ts` - Exports\n5. Tests\n\nLet me write efficient, clean code.\n\nFor the tokenizer, I'll handle:\n- Skip whitespace\n- Numbers (int or decimal): /\\d+(\\.\\d+)?/\n- Strings: /\"[^\"]*\"/\n- Booleans: true, false (but careful: these could be identifiers too)\n- Cell references: /[A-Za-z]+\\d+/\n- Identifiers: /[A-Za-z_]\\w*/\n- Two-char operators: <>, >=, <=\n- Single-char operators and punctuation: +, -, *, /, =, >, <, (, ), :, ,\n\nActually, in the tokenizer I need to be careful about the order:\n1. Try two-char operators first (>=, <=, <>)\n2. Then single-char operators\n3. Then numbers\n4. Then strings\n5. Then cell references (letter+digits)\n6. Then identifiers/keywords\n\nWait, there's an issue: \"A1\" is a cell reference (letter + digits), but \"SUM\" is an identifier (letters only, no digits), and \"TRUE\" could be a boolean keyword. Let me make `TRUE`, `FALSE` be reserved words that tokenize as BOOLEAN.\n\nIn the tokenizer:\n1. Skip whitespace\n2. Check for two-char ops: >=, <=, <>\n3. Check for single-char ops: +, -, *, /, =, >, <, (, ), :, ,\n4. Check for strings: \"\n5. Check for numbers: digit or .\n6. Check for letters/_: could be cell ref (if followed by digits) or identifier/boolean\n\nLet me code it:\n\n```typescript\nfunction tokenize(input: string): Token[] {\n  const tokens: Token[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  \n  while (i < input.length) {\n    // Skip whitespace\n    if (/\\s/.test(input[i])) { i++; continue; }\n    \n    // Two-char operators\n    if (input[i] === '>' && input[i+1] === '=') { tokens.push({ type: 'GREATER_EQUAL' }); i += 2; continue; }\n    if (input[i] === '<' && input[i+1] === '=') { tokens.push({ type: 'LESS_EQUAL' }); i += 2; continue; }\n    if (input[i] === '<' && input[i+1] === '>') { tokens.push({ type: 'NOT_EQUAL' }); i += 2; continue; }\n    \n    // Single-char operators\n    switch (input[i]) {\n      case '+': tokens.push({ type: 'PLUS' }); i++; continue;\n      case '-': tokens.push({ type: 'MINUS' }); i++; continue;\n      case '*': tokens.push({ type: 'STAR' }); i++; continue;\n      case '/': tokens.push({ type: 'SLASH' }); i++; continue;\n      case '=': tokens.push({ type: 'EQUAL' }); i++; continue;\n      case '>': tokens.push({ type: 'GREATER' }); i++; continue;\n      case '<': tokens.push({ type: 'LESS' }); i++; continue;\n      case '(': tokens.push({ type: 'LEFT_PAREN' }); i++; continue;\n      case ')': tokens.push({ type: 'RIGHT_PAREN' }); i++; continue;\n      case ':': tokens.push({ type: 'COLON' }); i++; continue;\n      case ',': tokens.push({ type: 'COMMA' }); i++; continue;\n    }\n    \n    // String\n    if (input[i] === '\"') {\n      let s = '';\n      i++; // skip opening \"\n      while (i < input.length && input[i] !== '\"') {\n        s += input[i];\n        i++;\n      }\n      i++; // skip closing \"\n      tokens.push({ type: 'STRING', value: s });\n      continue;\n    }\n    \n    // Number\n    if (/\\d/.test(input[i]) || (input[i] === '.' && i+1 < input.length && /\\d/.test(input[i+1]))) {\n      let num = '';\n      while (i < input.length && /\\d/.test(input[i])) { num += input[i]; i++; }\n      if (input[i] === '.') { num += '.'; i++; while (i < input.length && /\\d/.test(input[i])) { num += input[i]; i++; } }\n      tokens.push({ type: 'NUMBER', value: parseFloat(num) });\n      continue;\n    }\n    \n    // Identifier or cell reference\n    if (/[A-Za-z_]/.test(input[i])) {\n      let word = '';\n      while (i < input.length && /[A-Za-z0-9_]/.test(input[i])) { word += input[i]; i++; }\n      \n      if (word.toUpperCase() === 'TRUE') {\n        tokens.push({ type: 'BOOLEAN', value: true });\n      } else if (word.toUpperCase() === 'FALSE') {\n        tokens.push({ type: 'BOOLEAN', value: false });\n      } else if (/^[A-Za-z]+\\d+$/.test(word)) {\n        tokens.push({ type: 'CELL_REF', value: word.toUpperCase() });\n      } else {\n        tokens.push({ type: 'IDENTIFIER', value: word.toUpperCase() });\n      }\n      continue;\n    }\n    \n    throw new Error(`Unexpected character: ${input[i]}`);\n  }\n  \n  tokens.push({ type: 'EOF' });\n  return tokens;\n}\n```\n\nWait, I have a potential issue with the identifier/cell-ref distinction. Consider something like `A1B2`. Is that a cell ref `A1` followed by `B2`, or an identifier `A1B2`? In my current tokenizer, `A1B2` would match as an identifier (since it's letters+digits+letters... wait, my regex `/[A-Za-z0-9_]/` would match all of it as one word. Then the check `/^[A-Za-z]+\\d+$/` would fail because of the extra letters. So it would be tokenized as an IDENTIFIER. That's probably fine for now.\n\nBut what about `A1B2`? In practice, spreadsheets don't have identifiers like that. This would be a syntax error at the parser level anyway.\n\nAlso, there's an edge case: what about cell references with multiple letter columns like `AA1`? The regex `[A-Za-z]+\\d+` handles that fine.\n\nOK, let me now think about the parser. I'll implement a recursive descent parser.\n\n```typescript\nclass Parser {\n  private tokens: Token[];\n  private current: number = 0;\n  \n  constructor(tokens: Token[]) {\n    this.tokens = tokens;\n  }\n  \n  parse(): Expr {\n    const expr = this.expression();\n    if (!this.check('EOF')) {\n      throw new Error('Unexpected token after expression');\n    }\n    return expr;\n  }\n  \n  private expression(): Expr {\n    return this.equality();\n  }\n  \n  private equality(): Expr {\n    let left = this.comparison();\n    while (this.match('EQUAL', 'NOT_EQUAL')) {\n      const op = this.previous().type;\n      const right = this.comparison();\n      left = { type: 'binary', op: op === 'EQUAL' ? '=' : '<>', left, right };\n    }\n    return left;\n  }\n  \n  private comparison(): Expr {\n    let left = this.addition();\n    while (this.match('GREATER', 'GREATER_EQUAL', 'LESS', 'LESS_EQUAL')) {\n      const op = this.previous().type;\n      const right = this.addition();\n      left = { type: 'binary', op: op === 'GREATER' ? '>' : op === 'GREATER_EQUAL' ? '>=' : op === 'LESS' ? '<' : '<=', left, right };\n    }\n    return left;\n  }\n  \n  private addition(): Expr {\n    let left = this.multiplication();\n    while (this.match('PLUS', 'MINUS')) {\n      const op = this.previous().type;\n      const right = this.multiplication();\n      left = { type: 'binary', op: op === 'PLUS' ? '+' : '-', left, right };\n    }\n    return left;\n  }\n  \n  private multiplication(): Expr {\n    let left = this.unary();\n    while (this.match('STAR', 'SLASH')) {\n      const op = this.previous().type;\n      const right = this.unary();\n      left = { type: 'binary', op: op === 'STAR' ? '*' : '/', left, right };\n    }\n    return left;\n  }\n  \n  private unary(): Expr {\n    if (this.match('MINUS', 'NOT')) {\n      const op = this.previous().type;\n      const operand = this.unary();\n      return { type: 'unary', op: op === 'MINUS' ? '-' : '!', operand };\n    }\n    return this.primary();\n  }\n  \n  private primary(): Expr {\n    if (this.match('NUMBER')) {\n      return { type: 'number', value: this.previous().value };\n    }\n    if (this.match('STRING')) {\n      return { type: 'string', value: this.previous().value };\n    }\n    if (this.match('BOOLEAN')) {\n      return { type: 'boolean', value: this.previous().value };\n    }\n    if (this.match('LEFT_PAREN')) {\n      const expr = this.expression();\n      this.consume('RIGHT_PAREN', \"Expect ')' after expression.\");\n      return expr;\n    }\n    if (this.match('CELL_REF')) {\n      const ref = this.previous().value;\n      if (this.match('COLON')) {\n        const endRef = this.consume('CELL_REF', \"Expect cell reference after ':'.\").value;\n        return { type: 'range', start: ref, end: endRef };\n      }\n      return { type: 'cellRef', ref };\n    }\n    if (this.match('IDENTIFIER')) {\n      const name = this.previous().value;\n      this.consume('LEFT_PAREN', `Expect '(' after function name ${name}.`);\n      const args: Expr[] = [];\n      if (!this.check('RIGHT_PAREN')) {\n        do {\n          args.push(this.expression());\n        } while (this.match('COMMA'));\n      }\n      this.consume('RIGHT_PAREN', \"Expect ')' after arguments.\");\n      return { type: 'call', name, args };\n    }\n    throw new Error(`Unexpected token: ${this.peek().type}`);\n  }\n  \n  // Helper methods: match, check, consume, peek, previous\n}\n```\n\nNow for the evaluator:\n\n```typescript\ntype EvalContext = {\n  getCellValue: (ref: string) => any;\n};\n\nfunction evaluate(expr: Expr, context: EvalContext): any {\n  switch (expr.type) {\n    case 'number': return expr.value;\n    case 'string': return expr.value;\n    case 'boolean': return expr.value;\n    case 'cellRef': return context.getCellValue(expr.ref);\n    case 'range': {\n      const cells = expandRange(`${expr.start}:${expr.end}`);\n      return cells.map(cell => {\n        const val = context.getCellValue(cell);\n        return val;\n      });\n    }\n    case 'binary': {\n      const left = evaluate(expr.left, context);\n      const right = evaluate(expr.right, context);\n      return applyBinary(expr.op, left, right);\n    }\n    case 'unary': {\n      const operand = evaluate(expr.operand, context);\n      return applyUnary(expr.op, operand);\n    }\n    case 'call': {\n      const args = expr.args.map(arg => evaluate(arg, context));\n      return applyFunction(expr.name, args);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction applyBinary(op: string, left: any, right: any): any {\n  switch (op) {\n    case '+': return toNumber(left) + toNumber(right);\n    case '-': return toNumber(left) - toNumber(right);\n    case '*': return toNumber(left) * toNumber(right);\n    case '/': return toNumber(left) / toNumber(right);\n    case '=': return left === right; // or == ?\n    case '<>': return left !== right;\n    case '>': return toNumber(left) > toNumber(right);\n    case '>=': return toNumber(left) >= toNumber(right);\n    case '<': return toNumber(left) < toNumber(right);\n    case '<=': return toNumber(left) <= toNumber(right);\n    default: throw new Error(`Unknown operator: ${op}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, for comparison operators on strings: in spreadsheets, `>` etc. compare numerically. But `=` and `<>` compare values. Hmm, let me handle comparisons by: if both values are strings, compare lexicographically; otherwise convert to numbers.\n\nActually, in real spreadsheets like Excel, comparison operators work like:\n- `=` and `<>`: compare values of any type\n- `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`: compare numbers, or compare strings lexicographically if both are strings\n\nLet me implement it simply but correctly for the test cases we have.\n\nFor `toNumber`: if the value is null or undefined, return 0. If it's a string that can be parsed as a number, parse it. Otherwise return 0 or NaN.\n\nLet me be more careful:\n\n```typescript\nfunction toNumber(val: any): number {\n  if (val === null || val === undefined) return 0;\n  if (typeof val === 'number') return val;\n  if (typeof val === 'boolean') return val ? 1 : 0;\n  if (typeof val === 'string') {\n    const n = parseFloat(val);\n    return isNaN(n) ? 0 : n;\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nOK, now the spreadsheet engine:\n\n```typescript\nclass Spreadsheet {\n  private rawValues: Map<string, string | number | boolean> = new Map();\n  private formulas: Map<string, string> = new Map();\n  private parsedFormulas: Map<string, Expr> = new Map();\n  private computedValues: Map<string, any> = new Map();\n  \n  // depGraph[X] = set of cells that X depends on\n  private depGraph: Map<string, Set<string>> = new Map();\n  // reverseDep[X] = set of cells that depend on X\n  private reverseDep: Map<string, Set<string>> = new Map();\n  \n  set(cell: string, value: string | number | boolean): void {\n    const normalized = cell.toUpperCase();\n    \n    if (typeof value === 'string' && value.startsWith('=')) {\n      // Formula\n      const formula = value;\n      const tokens = tokenize(formula.substring(1));\n      const parser = new Parser(tokens);\n      const ast = parser.parse();\n      \n      // Extract cell references from AST\n      const deps = extractCellRefs(ast);\n      \n      // Check for circular dependency\n      if (this.wouldCreateCycle(normalized, deps)) {\n        throw new Error(`Circular reference detected: ${normalized}`);\n      }\n      \n      // Store formula\n      this.formulas.set(normalized, formula);\n      this.parsedFormulas.set(normalized, ast);\n      this.rawValues.set(normalized, formula);\n      \n      // Update dependency graph\n      this.updateDependencies(normalized, deps);\n      \n      // Evaluate and store\n      const context = this.createContext();\n      // For evaluation, the formula cell's old value should be used if it's referenced... no wait\n      // Actually for evaluating, we use current computed values. If the formula cell references itself (which we just prevented), it would be a problem.\n      const result = evaluate(ast, context);\n      this.computedValues.set(normalized, result);\n      \n      // Recompute dependents\n      this.recompute(normalized);\n    } else {\n      // Raw value\n      this.rawValues.set(normalized, value);\n      this.formulas.delete(normalized);\n      this.parsedFormulas.delete(normalized);\n      this.computedValues.set(normalized, value);\n      \n      // Remove old dependencies\n      this.updateDependencies(normalized, new Set());\n      \n      // Recompute dependents\n      this.recompute(normalized);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  private updateDependencies(cell: string, deps: Set<string>): void {\n    // Remove old dependencies\n    const oldDeps = this.depGraph.get(cell);\n    if (oldDeps) {\n      for (const dep of oldDeps) {\n        this.reverseDep.get(dep)?.delete(cell);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Set new dependencies\n    this.depGraph.set(cell, new Set(deps));\n    for (const dep of deps) {\n      if (!this.reverseDep.has(dep)) {\n        this.reverseDep.set(dep, new Set());\n      }\n      this.reverseDep.get(dep)!.add(cell);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  private wouldCreateCycle(cell: string, deps: Set<string>): boolean {\n    // Check if there's a path from any dep back to cell\n    // by following the dependency graph\n    for (const dep of deps) {\n      // BFS from dep\n      const queue = [dep];\n      const visited = new Set<string>();\n      \n      while (queue.length > 0) {\n        const current = queue.shift()!;\n        if (current === cell) return true;\n        if (visited.has(current)) continue;\n        visited.add(current);\n        \n        const nextDeps = this.depGraph.get(current);\n        if (nextDeps) {\n          queue.push(...nextDeps);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    return false;\n  }\n  \n  private recompute(changedCell: string): void {\n    // Find all cells that depend on changedCell (transitively)\n    const affected = new Set<string>();\n    const queue = [...(this.reverseDep.get(changedCell) || [])];\n    \n    while (queue.length > 0) {\n      const cell = queue.shift()!;\n      if (affected.has(cell)) continue;\n      affected.add(cell);\n      \n      const deps = this.reverseDep.get(cell);\n      if (deps) {\n        queue.push(...deps);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Topological sort of affected cells for correct evaluation order\n    const sorted = this.topologicalSort(affected);\n    \n    // Recompute each\n    const context = this.createContext();\n    for (const cell of sorted) {\n      const ast = this.parsedFormulas.get(cell);\n      if (ast) {\n        this.computedValues.set(cell, evaluate(ast, context));\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  private topologicalSort(cells: Set<string>): string[] {\n    // Kahn's algorithm\n    const inDegree = new Map<string, number>();\n    const graph = new Map<string, Set<string>>();\n    \n    // Only consider edges between cells in the set\n    for (const cell of cells) {\n      if (!inDegree.has(cell)) inDegree.set(cell, 0);\n      if (!graph.has(cell)) graph.set(cell, new Set());\n      \n      const deps = this.depGraph.get(cell);\n      if (deps) {\n        for (const dep of deps) {\n          if (cells.has(dep)) {\n            // edge from dep → cell (dep must be computed before cell)\n            graph.get(dep)!.add(cell);\n            inDegree.set(cell, (inDegree.get(cell) || 0) + 1);\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Start with cells that have no dependencies within the affected set\n    const queue: string[] = [];\n    for (const cell of cells) {\n      if (inDegree.get(cell) === 0) {\n        queue.push(cell);\n      }\n    }\n    \n    const result: string[] = [];\n    while (queue.length > 0) {\n      const cell = queue.shift()!;\n      result.push(cell);\n      \n      for (const neighbor of graph.get(cell) || []) {\n        const deg = inDegree.get(neighbor)! - 1;\n        inDegree.set(neighbor, deg);\n        if (deg === 0) {\n          queue.push(neighbor);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    return result;\n  }\n  \n  get(cell: string): any {\n    const normalized = cell.toUpperCase();\n    if (this.computedValues.has(normalized)) {\n      return this.computedValues.get(normalized);\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n  \n  getDisplay(cell: string): string {\n    const val = this.get(cell);\n    if (val === null || val === undefined) return '';\n    return String(val);\n  }\n  \n  getCells(): Map<string, any> {\n    return new Map(this.computedValues);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, I have an issue with the topological sort. The edges should go from DEPENDENCY → DEPENDENT, meaning if B1 depends on A1 (B1 = A1 + 1), then A1 must be computed before B1. In my graph:\n\ndepGraph['B1'] = {'A1'} means B1 depends on A1.\nreverseDep['A1'] = {'B1'} means A1 is needed by B1.\n\nFor topological sort, I want to order cells so that dependencies come before dependents. The edges should go from A1 → B1 (A1 before B1).\n\nSo in topologicalSort:\n- For each cell in the affected set, look at its dependencies (depGraph[cell])\n- If a dependency is also in the affected set, add edge: dep → cell\n\nThis is what I have. Let me trace through an example:\n\nA1 = 10, B1 = A1 + 1, C1 = B1 * 2\n\ndepGraph['B1'] = {'A1'}\ndepGraph['C1'] = {'B1'}\nreverseDep['A1'] = {'B1'}\nreverseDep['B1'] = {'C1'}\n\nIf A1 changes, affected = {'B1', 'C1'}.\n\nIn topologicalSort with cells = {'B1', 'C1'}:\n- B1: deps = {'A1'}. A1 is NOT in affected set. So no edges within set. inDegree = 0.\n- C1: deps = {'B1'}. B1 IS in affected set. So edge B1 → C1. inDegree = 1.\n\nQueue starts with B1 (inDegree 0). Process B1, then C1.\nResult: ['B1', 'C1']\n\nExcellent! Wait, but A1 is not in the affected set. So when evaluating B1 and C1, they'll use the current computed values. But A1's value is already updated (we set it before recomputing). The context reads from computedValues, which has A1's new value. So B1 would compute correctly.\n\nActually wait, I need to double-check: when we set A1 to a new value, we update computedValues[A1] first, then call recompute('A1'). The affected set includes B1 and C1. But the topological sort only includes cells in the affected set (B1, C1). Since B1 depends on A1 (which is not in affected), B1 will be at the front of the queue. When evaluating B1, the context reads A1's value from computedValues, which is the new value. Good.\n\nBut what about this case: X1 = Y1, Y1 = Z1, Z1 = 0\n\nIf I change Z1 to 5: Z1 is updated, affected = {Y1, X1}. Topological sort: Y1 (depends on Z1 which is not in affected) comes first. Good. Then X1 (depends on Y1 which IS in affected, so after Y1). Result: ['Y1', 'X1']. \n\nBut what about: X1 = Y1 + Z1, Y1 = Z1, Z1 = 0\n\ndepGraph['X1'] = {'Y1', 'Z1'}\ndepGraph['Y1'] = {'Z1'}\nreverseDep['Z1'] = {'Y1', 'X1'}\nreverseDep['Y1'] = {'X1'}\n\nIf Z1 changes: affected = {Y1, X1}\nTopological sort:\n- Y1: deps = {'Z1'}. Z1 not in affected. inDegree(Y1) = 0.\n- X1: deps = {'Y1', 'Z1'}. Y1 is in affected. edge Y1 → X1. Z1 not in affected. inDegree(X1) = 