risons, I'll convert both to numbers.\n\nActually, wait. In spreadsheets, `=` in formulas is value equality, not strict equality. So `= \"5\" = 5` might be true? In Excel, `\"5\"=5` is TRUE because Excel coerces the string to a number. Let me just use `==` for the comparison operator... Actually no, let me use strict equality for simplicity and correctness. The spec doesn't detail this.\n\nHmm, actually using strict === for = and !== for <> is fine. Order comparisons I'll convert to numbers.\n\nActually, looking at spreadsheet semantics more carefully:\n- `=A1=B1` compares A1 and B1 with value equality\n- This usually involves type coercion\n\nFor simplicity, I'll use `==` for the = comparison and `!=` for <>. No, that's not great TypeScript. Let me use loose comparison:\n\n```typescript\ncase '=': return left == right;  // loose equality\ncase '<>': return left != right; // loose inequality\n```\n\nThis handles `\"5\" == 5` as true, which is spreadsheet-like behavior. Actually, let me reconsider. The test examples don't test this edge case. Let me just use strict equality for simplicity:\n\n```typescript\ncase '=': return left === right;\ncase '<>': return left !== right;\n```\n\nIf someone writes `=A1=10`, and A1 is the number 10, `10 === 10` is true. Good. If A1 is the string \"10\", `\"10\" === 10` is false. That might be surprising, but it's fine for this exercise.\n\nActually, in a spreadsheet, if a cell contains the number 10, `=A1=10` would evaluate to true. If the cell contains the string \"10\", it would... well, that depends on the spreadsheet. Let me just go with strict equality.\n\nAlright, let me write the code! I'll be thorough but practical.\n\nLet me also reconsider the structure. I want clean, well-organized code. Here's my final plan:\n\n1. `src/reference.ts` — Cell reference parsing and range expansion\n2. `src/tokenizer.ts` — Formula tokenizer\n3. `src/parser.ts` — Recursive descent parser producing AST\n4. `src/types.ts` — Shared types\n5. `src/evaluator.ts` — AST evaluator with function support\n6. `src/spreadsheet.ts` — Main Spreadsheet class with dependency tracking\n7. `src/index.ts` — Public exports\n8. Tests\n\nActually, let me keep it simpler with fewer files. I'll go with:\n1. `src/reference.ts` — Cell reference utilities\n2. `src/formula.ts` — Tokenizer, parser, evaluator all together\n3. `src/spreadsheet.ts` — Spreadsheet engine\n4. `src/index.ts` — Exports\n\nOK let me just write it now. Enough planning!\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about what happens when a formula references a cell that has a formula that hasn't been evaluated yet. In our implementation, all formulas are evaluated immediately when set(). So this shouldn't be an issue. But during recomputation (when a dependency changes), we evaluate in topological order, so dependencies are always evaluated first.\n\nWait, there's a subtle issue. When we call `evaluate(ast, context)` during recompute, the context reads from computedValues. If we're evaluating B1 (which depends on A1), we need A1's up-to-date value. Since we recompute in topological order, A1 (or whatever dependency) should already have been recomputed if it's in the affected set, or it wasn't affected and still has its old (correct) value. So this should work.\n\nLet me also handle the case where a cell is set to a formula that references cells that themselves have formulas. For instance:\n\n```\nA1 = 10\nB1 = A1 * 2\nC1 = B1 + 5\n```\n\nWhen setting C1 = B1 + 5:\n- Parse formula, extract deps: {B1}\n- Check for cycles: B1 → A1, neither reaches C1. Good.\n- Evaluate: getCellValue('B1') = computedValues['B1'] = 20. So C1 = 25.\n\nNow change A1 to 20:\n- Update computedValues['A1'] = 20\n- Recompute('A1'): affected = {B1, C1}\n  - Topological sort: B1, C1\n  - Evaluate B1: A1 = 20, B1 = 40\n  - Evaluate C1: B1 = 40 (just computed), C1 = 45\n\n\n\nNow one more thing: the getCellValue callback in the context. During recomputation, when evaluating cell X, the context